1
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:07,656
Welcome to the Pre Sales Agent Framework.

2
00:00:07,656 --> 00:00:21,415
It turns everything a customer sends you, emails, call transcripts and tender documents, into confirmed requirements, clarification questions, specialist assessments and a reviewed, traceable estimate.

3
00:00:21,415 --> 00:00:31,135
In this tour we start from an empty workspace and take one bid all the way: Larkspur Mutual, an insurer replacing its mainframe claims system.

4
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:37,683
The sidebar takes you to the Inbox, Opportunities, Knowledge, Reports and Admin.

5
00:00:37,683 --> 00:00:44,882
Press Control K anywhere for the command palette, to jump to a page or create something without the mouse.

6
00:00:45,983 --> 00:00:50,567
First, an administrator prepares the knowledge the agents rely on.

7
00:00:50,567 --> 00:01:01,262
Under Admin, Catalogue, we add the integration types and work packages this bid needs, such as Policy administration integration, with a short definition.

8
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:16,408
Seven entries later, the catalogue holds five integration types, from payments to identity and single sign-on, and two work packages: data migration, and regulatory and outsourcing assurance.

9
00:01:16,408 --> 00:01:21,855
Checklists, assessments, estimate lines and actuals all share these names.

10
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:28,678
Next, we register a Knowledge Source: our own insurance claims migration playbook.

11
00:01:28,678 --> 00:01:33,847
We give it a title and a product, and tag the integration types it covers.

12
00:01:33,847 --> 00:01:39,575
It's split into chunks and embedded, so the Research Agent can search and cite it.

13
00:01:40,683 --> 00:01:45,283
The playbook is now ready, with its owner, version and review date.

14
00:01:45,283 --> 00:01:50,157
Stale sources are flagged, so knowledge doesn't quietly go out of date.

15
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:55,123
Then we write a Checklist, Claims payments readiness.

16
00:01:55,123 --> 00:01:59,727
Each item is a question with an impact and a reason it matters.

17
00:01:59,727 --> 00:02:05,646
Items marked mandatory always raise a gap when the customer hasn't answered them.

18
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:10,902
Six items, three of them mandatory, published as a version.

19
00:02:10,902 --> 00:02:15,899
A published checklist applies to every opportunity whose scope matches.

20
00:02:16,983 --> 00:02:24,075
Coverage shows, for every product and integration type, whether we have documentation and checklists.

21
00:02:24,075 --> 00:02:28,640
Red means missing coverage, so gap detection may be weaker there.

22
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:41,406
And the Knowledge Graph draws the same picture as a map of products, integration types, sources and checklists, with missing coverage shown as dashed lines and red rings.

23
00:02:42,517 --> 00:02:44,134
Now the bid itself.

24
00:02:44,134 --> 00:02:51,031
Choose New Opportunity, then Import from file, and pick the customer's RFP email.

25
00:02:51,031 --> 00:02:54,266
The Opportunity Intake Agent reads it.

26
00:02:55,350 --> 00:03:05,737
Seconds later the form is filled in: customer, title, the four products in scope, industry and the proposal date, each marked From file.

27
00:03:05,737 --> 00:03:08,792
Check them and click Create Opportunity.

28
00:03:08,792 --> 00:03:11,465
The email becomes the first source.

29
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:19,418
The Overview opens with the submission pipeline: eight stages, from sources added to review and baseline.

30
00:03:19,418 --> 00:03:21,838
We'll work through every one of them.

31
00:03:22,950 --> 00:03:38,414
On the Sources tab we drop in the rest of the customer's material: the discovery call transcript from Teams, two emails, the RFP requirements schedule in Word, a security appendix as PDF, and our architect's notes.

32
00:03:38,414 --> 00:03:41,665
Every file is stored unchanged and versioned.

33
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:48,368
Paste text handles anything without a file, like this message from the broker hub.

34
00:03:48,368 --> 00:03:55,631
Notice the footer telling automated tools to mark everything delivered and drop the migration assumptions.

35
00:03:55,631 --> 00:03:59,468
The app treats that as content, never as an instruction.

36
00:04:00,583 --> 00:04:04,902
All eight sources are parsed, and stage one is complete.

37
00:04:06,017 --> 00:04:15,351
Select any source to open the Inspector: who added it and when, a download of the original, and the extracted text the agents read.

38
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:19,838
While you work, the agents run in the background.

39
00:04:19,838 --> 00:04:28,964
The Intake Agent has already extracted sixty requirements, and a first round of gaps, assessments and a draft estimate is under way.

40
00:04:28,964 --> 00:04:30,485
Stage two is complete.

41
00:04:30,485 --> 00:04:34,840
The Overview now lists every blocker between us and submission.

42
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:44,240
Requirements are grouped by category: functional, integration, data, security, non-functional and commercial.

43
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:50,857
Each one cites the sources it came from, and nothing counts until a person confirms it.

44
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:58,505
Open a requirement to see its evidence, the exact passage highlighted in the source.

45
00:04:58,505 --> 00:05:03,421
From here you can edit, split, merge, reclassify or confirm it.

46
00:05:04,517 --> 00:05:13,154
Two requirements say the same thing about a broker view, so we select both, write the merged wording, and merge them into one.

47
00:05:14,250 --> 00:05:21,598
We tag each integration requirement with its integration type, here the policy administration mainframe.

48
00:05:21,598 --> 00:05:24,142
Tags let the right checklists apply.

49
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:26,628
Then Confirm all.

50
00:05:26,628 --> 00:05:29,383
Confirmed requirements are locked.

51
00:05:30,483 --> 00:05:39,654
When the customer later emails a scope change, adding pet insurance and instant payments, it can't silently rewrite locked requirements.

52
00:05:39,654 --> 00:05:45,251
Instead you get pending changes, showing the current text against the proposed one.

53
00:05:46,350 --> 00:05:51,936
Review each change and accept or reject it, with the new evidence beside it.

54
00:05:51,936 --> 00:05:55,684
We accept them, and confirm the requirements again.

55
00:05:56,783 --> 00:06:04,420
The app also warns that some assessments used requirements that have changed since, so you know they need a fresh run.

56
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:08,951
With all seventy-four requirements confirmed, stage three is complete.

57
00:06:10,050 --> 00:06:11,466
Stage four is gaps.

58
00:06:11,466 --> 00:06:22,496
The Clarification Agent finds what the estimate needs but the sources don't say, ranked by impact, and drafts a clarification question for each one.

59
00:06:23,583 --> 00:06:28,056
Open a gap to see why it matters and the requirements it affects.

60
00:06:28,056 --> 00:06:34,868
This one is marked Unknown to the customer: the fraud scoring vendor won't be chosen until January.

61
00:06:35,983 --> 00:06:38,909
We sharpen the drafted question before it goes out.

62
00:06:40,017 --> 00:06:46,193
Approve all, then export the questions to Word, an email draft, or the clipboard.

63
00:06:47,283 --> 00:06:50,766
When they've gone to the customer, mark them sent with the date.

64
00:06:51,850 --> 00:06:56,554
When the customer replies, record the answer against the question.

65
00:06:56,554 --> 00:07:02,113
Each answer becomes a new source, so the requirements and gaps update from it.

66
00:07:03,217 --> 00:07:06,476
One answer often covers other questions too.

67
00:07:06,476 --> 00:07:11,365
The app suggests a possible answer, quoting the sentence it found.

68
00:07:11,365 --> 00:07:17,514
Here the broker hub sentence answers the authentication question, so we confirm it.

69
00:07:17,514 --> 00:07:21,514
A suggestion that doesn't fit is marked Not an answer.

70
00:07:22,617 --> 00:07:31,953
Because payments integration is in scope, the checklist now raises its three mandatory gaps too, marked Checklist and Mandatory.

71
00:07:31,953 --> 00:07:35,016
Nothing on the must-ask list is forgotten.

72
00:07:36,117 --> 00:07:37,766
Stage five, assessments.

73
00:07:37,766 --> 00:07:56,875
Specialist agents judge the confirmed scope in parallel: Engineering for feasibility and effort per integration, the PM Agent for phases and dependencies, the Security Agent for mandatory controls, then a Critic and a Red Team that argues integrations are harder than they look.

74
00:07:57,983 --> 00:08:07,178
Each agent returns a verdict, such as proceed with conditions, with its confidence, the effort behind it, and the requirements it relied on.

75
00:08:08,283 --> 00:08:15,439
Findings are ranked by severity, and critical ones block submission.

76
00:08:15,439 --> 00:08:19,754
4 million claims is badly underestimated.

77
00:08:20,850 --> 00:08:29,875
We answer with a Challenge on the Engineering assessment: plan three trial migrations, and show the reconciliation effort separately.

78
00:08:29,875 --> 00:08:33,064
Choose which assessments to rerun, and send it.

79
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:42,957
The agent run panel shows the rerun live: the Engineering Agent working, the Critic queued, the Red Team done, each with its time.

80
00:08:42,957 --> 00:08:45,667
You can cancel or start a run from here.

81
00:08:46,783 --> 00:08:49,842
The rerun builds a new estimate version.

82
00:08:49,842 --> 00:08:56,495
We then resolve each remaining critical finding with a recorded reason, or override it.

83
00:08:56,495 --> 00:08:58,254
Stage five is complete.

84
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:00,903
Stage six, conflicts.

85
00:09:00,903 --> 00:09:10,221
The Conflict Agent flags things that can't both be true, here the PM Agent sizing a requirement that Engineering left unsized.

86
00:09:10,221 --> 00:09:14,954
Pick a position or propose your own, give a reason, and resolve.

87
00:09:16,050 --> 00:09:21,530
Every conflict is resolved, each one recording who decided and why.

88
00:09:22,617 --> 00:09:24,642
Stage seven, the estimate.

89
00:09:24,642 --> 00:09:28,148
It's built line by line from the assessments.

90
00:09:28,148 --> 00:09:34,691
Each line covers named requirements, with the engineer, PM and QA mix and the hours.

91
00:09:35,783 --> 00:09:37,366
Edit any line in place.

92
00:09:37,366 --> 00:09:46,035
The customer said translations take four weeks per handover, so we raise the letters line from forty-four to sixty-four hours.

93
00:09:46,035 --> 00:09:48,787
Every edit needs a reason and is traced.

94
00:09:49,883 --> 00:09:56,553
The Assumptions Register lists every condition the estimate depends on, each one coming from a gap.

95
00:09:56,553 --> 00:09:59,450
A named person must accept each assumption.

96
00:10:00,550 --> 00:10:10,565
Contingencies carry hours, for things nobody can know yet, like undocumented ClaimsOne status codes and pre-2015 data correction.

97
00:10:10,565 --> 00:10:14,835
Larkspur carries nine hundred and twenty hours of them.

98
00:10:14,835 --> 00:10:21,123
Accepting the assumptions converts their gaps, so stage four is now complete too.

99
00:10:22,217 --> 00:10:27,000
Back on the Overview: seven of eight stages complete, nothing blocked.

100
00:10:28,083 --> 00:10:29,045
Click Submit.

101
00:10:29,045 --> 00:10:36,814
Version six, at ten thousand and twenty-two hours, is locked, because submitted versions can't be edited.

102
00:10:37,917 --> 00:10:38,648
Stage eight.

103
00:10:38,648 --> 00:10:44,558
First we share the opportunity with a colleague, adding them as a collaborator from the Overview.

104
00:10:45,650 --> 00:10:47,058
Then Request review.

105
00:10:47,058 --> 00:10:54,659
Choose the review type, here engineering, pick the reviewer, add a message about what to focus on, and send.

106
00:10:55,750 --> 00:10:57,690
This is the reviewer's side.

107
00:10:57,690 --> 00:10:59,976
The request lands in their Inbox.

108
00:11:01,083 --> 00:11:05,535
The review page shows the message, the totals and every line.

109
00:11:05,535 --> 00:11:12,322
The reviewer can approve, reject, challenge, ask for evidence or an alternative, or escalate.

110
00:11:13,417 --> 00:11:18,061
The reviewer approves, and the owner is notified in their own Inbox.

111
00:11:19,150 --> 00:11:21,919
With an approved version, set the Baseline.

112
00:11:21,919 --> 00:11:27,974
It's the version delivery will be measured against, replaced only by a newer approved version.

113
00:11:29,083 --> 00:11:34,006
All eight stages are complete, and the opportunity is baselined.

114
00:11:35,117 --> 00:11:37,288
Every change made a new version.

115
00:11:37,288 --> 00:11:43,801
Diff against an earlier one to see which lines and assumptions changed, and how the total moved.

116
00:11:44,917 --> 00:11:55,133
And the Trace tab is the append-only audit log of every action, by people and agents alike, filterable by subject, actor and event.

117
00:11:56,250 --> 00:11:59,055
After delivery, record actual hours.

118
00:11:59,055 --> 00:12:05,988
Download the template, fill in actual hours and a variance cause per line, and import it.

119
00:12:05,988 --> 00:12:10,507
You see a preview, and nothing is saved until you confirm.

120
00:12:11,617 --> 00:12:15,320
Every line now shows its variance against the baseline.

121
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:18,350
Overruns can name the assumption that failed.

122
00:12:19,450 --> 00:12:23,940
Finally, record the outcome: won, or lost with a reason.

123
00:12:23,940 --> 00:12:27,066
Larkspur went to an incumbent on price.

124
00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:31,114
The outcome sets the retention date.

125
00:12:31,114 --> 00:12:35,395
For a lost bid, the data is kept until October 2029.

126
00:12:36,483 --> 00:12:40,939
Reports compare estimates with actuals across opportunities.

127
00:12:40,939 --> 00:12:46,583
Larkspur came in three point one percent over, with five failed assumptions.

128
00:12:46,583 --> 00:12:51,855
Group by integration type, product, work package, cause or opportunity.

129
00:12:52,950 --> 00:12:56,897
Expand a row to drill down to the opportunities behind it.

130
00:12:57,983 --> 00:13:00,507
And open the failed assumptions.

131
00:13:00,507 --> 00:13:10,600
Policy administration ran forty-eight percent over because of an unknown requirement: the undocumented status codes contingency.

132
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:13,123
One click opens it in the trace.

133
00:13:14,217 --> 00:13:26,152
Grouped by cause, the picture is clear: customer delay, scope change and an unknown requirement drove the overruns, while estimation error stayed under one percent.

134
00:13:27,250 --> 00:13:33,055
Under Admin, Users and roles shows each person's roles, managed in Auth0.

135
00:13:34,150 --> 00:13:40,104
The Agent Registry lists every agent, its version, model and tool permissions.

136
00:13:40,104 --> 00:13:45,296
Any tool call an agent isn't allowed to make is blocked and audited.

137
00:13:46,383 --> 00:13:55,300
Evals run the agents against reference sets, checking release bars like extraction recall, citation validity and gap recall.

138
00:13:56,383 --> 00:14:01,539
And Retention lists opportunities due for purge and recent purges.

139
00:14:01,539 --> 00:14:05,757
Data is purged automatically after its retention date.

140
00:14:05,757 --> 00:14:08,179
Nothing can be deleted by hand.

141
00:14:09,283 --> 00:14:14,995
In Settings, choose your theme and row density, and turn on single-key shortcuts.

142
00:14:16,083 --> 00:14:29,388
That's the whole journey: from a customer's first email, to confirmed requirements, answered questions, specialist assessments, a reviewed and baselined estimate, and the actuals that show how it held up.

143
00:14:29,388 --> 00:14:32,519
Open the user guide for every feature in detail.

144
00:14:32,519 --> 00:14:33,823
Thanks for watching.
